eammate gets to lead next, which is fine with me. That way, those singles remain.\n\nOption 2 would be to beat the Jack. This looks tempting because I want to win this round, so I would play the King or Ace. If I play the King or Ace, I force the play, and I\'d have to follow up with one of the single low cards that I have.  That\'s a trap I don\'t want to fall into.\n\nPlaying now wastes a high card and could put me in a weaker position.  I have 222 + RJ, I should only put them to use if I can close the game or my partner is in real trouble. But this isn\'t that. My role here is to support my partner.\n\nSo, the optimal strategy here is to *pass*. Let the Landlord spend a high card. This forces the Landlord to deal with the teammate\'s play, and I have the power to destroy the Landlord\'s next card. It also preserves my hand for later. It\'s a classic "wait and see" play, and in Dou Dizhu, that\'s often the best move, especially with a hand like mine.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My hand is very powerful with the Red Joker, three 2s, and a triplet of 10s, but it has poor structure for \'running\' due to the disconnected low singles (4, 5, 6, 7). My teammate has played a Jack, which beats the Landlord\'s 6. Since I play last in this sequence (Landlord -> Teammate -> Me), I am in the "Goalie" position. If I pass, the Landlord must either spend a high card (Q, K, A, or 2) to beat the Jack or let my teammate win the lead. Beating my teammate with a King or Ace would be a mistake because it wastes a high card and forces me to lead a weak single (like the 4) which limits my control. It is much better to let the Landlord struggle against the Jack and save my 2s and Joker to crush the Landlord\'s high cards later.\n\nReason: Teammate is winning the trick with a Jack. Blocking the teammate is unnecessary and counterproductive given my hand\'s need to clear low singles slowly. Passing puts pressure on the Landlord to respond.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
